W: 1. As I walked out one morning being in the blooming spring,
W: I heard a lovely maid complain, so grievously to sing;
W: Saying, "Cruel was my parents, they did me so annoy,
W: And will not let me marry with my bonny labouring boy."
W:
W: 2. Young Johnny was my true love's name as you may plainly see
W: My parents did employ him their labouring boy to be;
W: To harrow, reap, to sow the seed to plough my father's land
W: And soon I fell in love with him as you may understand.
W:
W: 3. My father stepped up one morning and he seized me by the hand
W: He swore he'd send young Johnny unto some foreign land.
W: He locked me in my bedroom, my comfort to annoy
W: And to keep me there to weep and morn for my bonny labouring boy.
W:
W: 4. My mother stepped up net morning these words to me did say:
W: "Your father has intended to appoint your wedding day,"
W: But I did not make no answer, bor I dared not complain
W: But until I wed my labouring boy then single I'll remain.
W:
W: 5. Oh, his cheeks are like the roses, his eyes as black as sloes
W: He smiles in his behaviour whever my love goes.
W: He's manly, neat and handsome, his skin as white as snow
W: In spite of my parents with my labouring boy I'll go.
W:
W: 6. So fill this glass up to the brim, let the toast come early round,
W: Here's a health to the labouring boy that ploughs and sows the ground;
W: And when his work is over, his home he will enjoy
W: Oh how happy is the girl that weds with a bonny labouring boy.
%
